-loop is weighed once, from the end spanned first: an edge"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"whose far end is already spanned is skipped before it is weighed."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"name":"Complexity","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"O(m log n), with a 4-ary indexed heap."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"type":"aside","style":"note"},{"name":"Precondition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"As for ","type":"text"},{"code":"minimumSpanningTree(weight:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]}],"type":"aside","style":"note"}]}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Prim’s algorithm over every component (NetworkX, JGraphT, igraph): grows a tree from the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"first unspanned vertex in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"type":"text","text":" order, joining the lightest edge out of it each step,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"and repeats until every vertex is spanned. A minimum spanning forest; among equal weights,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"which edges it chooses is unspecified (a canonical choice is measurably slower; see the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"benchmarks). Edges are listed in the order their far vertices joined, so each joins a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"spanned vertex to a new one; the weight is added up in that order."}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/spanningtrees\/graphprotocols\/graph\/primminimumspanningtree(weight:)"]}],"metadata":{"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"primMinimumSpanningTree","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"W","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":">(","kind":"text"},{"text":"weight","kind":"externalParam"},{"text":": (","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"